From a3d569699dc78dfd3974af1560b80d71cc1c505e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 23:48:27 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] rt11: translate '\n' to CR LF in _write() on the console

C's '\n' means "move to the start of the next line" (ISO C 5.2.2), and
a text stream may be translated to the host's convention to get there
(7.21.2).  RT-11's convention is CR LF and .TTYOUT sends exactly the
byte it is given, so a bare LF only moved the cursor down: every line
printed with puts()/printf()/"\n" started in the column where the
previous one ended.  A Unix tty driver adds the CR itself (ONLCR);
RT-11 has no such layer, so _write() now does it for fd 1 and 2, the
mirror image of the '\r' -> '\n' translation _read() already does on
input (ICRNL).  Programs keep writing plain "\n" and stay portable; an
explicit "\r\n" still works (CR twice, harmless on a terminal).

Also fix the comment in read.c, which claimed write.c already did this
translation -- it never had, in any revision of this port.

Verified in the ukncbtl-debugger emulator: puts(), printf(), fputs()
and a raw write() of "\n"-terminated lines all start at column 0.
---
 newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c  |  8 ++++----
 newlib/libc/sys/rt11/write.c | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 19 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c
index 0cdee7695..9bb742bb1 100644
--- a/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c
+++ b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/read.c
@@ -38,10 +38,10 @@ _read (int file, void *ptr, size_t len)
 	     will never get from this line -- confirmed directly: it stalls
 	     waiting past the Enter that already terminated the line,
 	     silently swallowing whatever key comes next into the same
-	     buffer.  Translating '\r' to '\n' here mirrors the LF->CRLF
-	     translation write.c already does symmetrically on output (the
-	     canonical terminal-driver "icrnl" behavior RT-11 itself
-	     doesn't provide) and fixes fgets() outright.  Safe for any
+	     buffer.  Translating '\r' to '\n' here is the canonical
+	     terminal-driver "icrnl" behavior RT-11 itself doesn't
+	     provide (write.c does the mirror-image LF -> CR LF on
+	     output, "onlcr") and fixes fgets() outright.  Safe for any
 	     caller that still checks for '\r' too (e.g. strcspn(buf,
 	     "\r\n")) -- '\n' alone already satisfies that check.  */
 	  if (c == '\r')
diff --git a/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/write.c b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/write.c
index cb495b426..ab97b9b1c 100644
--- a/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/write.c
+++ b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/write.c
@@ -30,8 +30,22 @@ _write (int file, const void *ptr, size_t len)
 
   if (file == 1 || file == 2)
     {
+      /* C's '\n' means "start of the next line" (ISO C 5.2.2), and a
+	 text stream may be translated to the host's convention to get
+	 there (7.21.2).  RT-11's convention is CR LF, and .TTYOUT sends
+	 exactly the byte it is given -- a bare LF just moves the cursor
+	 down, leaving it in the same column.  A Unix tty driver would
+	 add the CR itself (ONLCR); RT-11 has no such layer, so do it
+	 here, the mirror image of the '\r' -> '\n' translation read.c
+	 does on input (ICRNL).  Programs keep writing plain "\n" and
+	 stay portable; an explicit "\r\n" still works, it just sends CR
+	 twice, which is harmless on a terminal.  */
       for (i = 0; i < len; i++)
-	rt11_ttyout ((char) p[i]);
+	{
+	  if (p[i] == '\n')
+	    rt11_ttyout ('\r');
+	  rt11_ttyout ((char) p[i]);
+	}
       return (int) len;
     }
 
